<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>org.pragmatica-lite</groupId>
        <artifactId>pragmatica</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.0-rc4</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>script-gate</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>Shell Script Instrument Gate</name>
    <description>
        Test-only module. Owns no production code: it EXECUTES this repository's operator-facing shell
        scripts against constructed conditions and asserts they cannot report success without having
        looked.
    </description>

    <!--
        WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS.

        Two of this repository's scripts reported success while examining nothing. forge.sh ran the
        acceptance suite with `-pl` and no `-am`, so it could pass or fail against installed bytecode
        that was not the tree's (#865). start-postgres.sh printed "PostgreSQL ready" and exited 0 for
        a container that had died 295ms earlier, because its readiness loop had no failure branch
        (#952). Both are the same defect: an instrument whose success carries no information.

        A test that reads a script's TEXT cannot catch this class, because the defect is behavioural -
        the old readiness loop contains the string "PostgreSQL ready" in exactly the position the
        fixed one does. So these tests RUN the real scripts, with the container runtime and Maven
        stubbed on PATH, and assert on the specific diagnostic each one emits. Asserting a non-zero
        exit alone would be worthless here: "it failed correctly" and "the fixture never worked" are
        the same observation, which is why every refusal test is paired with a positive control that
        drives the same fixture to success.

        Running a script needs a process, not a classpath, so this module test-depends only on the
        harness and on `core`, whose Result/Option carry the IO outcomes. It links no BSL aether
        module, unlike cli-docs-gate, which explains its own BSL header by its test-scoped
        dependency on aether/cli; that is why this module carries no licence header. Its position in
        the root <modules> list is free, and it sits BEFORE cli-docs-gate so that module's "LAST,
        deliberately" stays true.

        THIS MODULE GATES ITSELF. Test sources are unlinted repo-wide - jbct.includeTests defaults to
        false - so every test-only module, INCLUDING the modules that exist to enforce gates, is
        examined by nothing. A gate module exempt from the gate is precisely the defect this module
        was built to catch, reproduced in the cure, so jbct.includeTests is true here.

        It is set on this module ALONE and deliberately not at the root: turning it on repo-wide is
        #938, held behind #974 pending an owner ruling. Turning it on cost a real rewrite rather than
        a flag - 43 lint errors and 6 format issues, almost all JBCT-EX-01 (`throws` on a JUnit
        method) - so the harness returns Result<T> from every IO operation and no test method
        declares a checked exception. That is why ScriptRunner and SyntheticRepo look like production
        code: under this property they are held to production rules.
    -->

    <properties>
        <!-- Gate-only module: it produces an empty jar and must never be published. -->
        <maven.deploy.skip>true</maven.deploy.skip>
        <gpg.skip>true</gpg.skip>

        <!-- Without BOTH of these the JBCT gate examines zero files here and exits green - the
             #740 WARNING says so out loud, and a gate module that ignores it is not a gate. -->
        <jbct.skip>false</jbct.skip>
        <jbct.includeTests>true</jbct.includeTests>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <!-- Result/Option carry every IO outcome in the harness, so no helper declares `throws`. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.pragmatica-lite</groupId>
            <artifactId>core</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
            <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
</project>
